@host = http://localhost:5080
@feed = http://localhost:5081
@season = {{seasonId}}

### The league and its fixture list, as the provider publishes them. Start here: the season identifier
### every other request needs is derived from the catalogue, and this is the readable way to find it.
GET {{feed}}/fixtures

### What is being played right now. This list is never cached: it changes by the second, and a client
### watching it is already being sent the changes.
GET {{host}}/matches/live

### The first page of a season's fixtures. Note the envelope: a value array, and a nextLink when there
### is more. Follow the link rather than building the next request yourself.
GET {{host}}/seasons/{{season}}/fixtures?$top=5

### Page two, by sending back the token from the nextLink above, exactly as it was given. It encodes
### the last row seen, so the next page is a seek and not a count.
GET {{host}}/seasons/{{season}}/fixtures?$top=5&$skiptoken=REPLACE_WITH_THE_TOKEN_FROM_NEXTLINK

### A token that cannot be read is refused rather than treated as the beginning. A client paging
### through a list would otherwise start again at the top and repeat everything it had read.
GET {{host}}/seasons/{{season}}/fixtures?$skiptoken=nonsense

### Page size is capped. Asking for five thousand rows gets a hundred, because the slowest query in
### the service should not be whatever somebody typed into a URL.
GET {{host}}/seasons/{{season}}/fixtures?$top=5000

### Results, newest first, which is the question a results list answers.
GET {{host}}/seasons/{{season}}/results?$top=5

### One match with its whole timeline. isDegraded is part of the contract: a score known to be missing
### an event is still shown, and it says so.
GET {{host}}/matches/REPLACE_WITH_A_MATCH_ID

### The league table. Ordered by points, then goal difference, then goals scored.
GET {{host}}/seasons/{{season}}/table

### The scorer list. Own goals move the score and credit nobody, so they are not here.
GET {{host}}/seasons/{{season}}/scorers?$top=10

### A team's recent results, from that team's point of view.
GET {{host}}/teams/REPLACE_WITH_A_TEAM_ID/form?seasonId={{season}}

### A first visit, which hands back an identity in an httpOnly cookie. Everything below needs it.
POST {{host}}/devices

### What this browser has chosen so far. Without the cookie this answers 401, because there is nobody
### to answer about.
GET {{host}}/devices/me

### Follow a team. Identifiers for teams that do not exist are ignored rather than stored.
PUT {{host}}/devices/me/favourites
Content-Type: application/json

{ "teamIds": ["REPLACE_WITH_A_TEAM_ID"] }

### How and when to be told. The zone is checked here rather than when a goal is scored, so a zone
### this service cannot resolve fails now instead of silencing notifications months later.
PUT {{host}}/devices/me/preferences
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "timeZoneId": "Europe/Istanbul",
  "quietHoursStart": "22:00:00",
  "quietHoursEnd": "08:00:00",
  "notifyOnGoal": true,
  "notifyOnRedCard": true,
  "notifyOnKickoff": false,
  "notifyOnFullTime": true
}

### The public half of the signing key, which a browser needs before it can subscribe.
GET {{host}}/push/public-key

### Where to reach this browser when its tab is closed. A browser re-subscribing sends the same
### endpoint, and it moves rather than adding a second row, so one goal never arrives twice.
POST {{host}}/devices/me/push-subscriptions
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "endpoint": "https://push.example/REPLACE",
  "keys": { "p256dh": "REPLACE", "auth": "REPLACE" }
}

### Liveness is about the process. Readiness is about whether it can reach PostgreSQL, and it is meant
### to be able to fail.
GET {{host}}/health/live

###
GET {{host}}/health/ready

### The ingestion boundary. Every request is signed over a length prefixed timestamp and the body, so
### this one cannot be sent by hand: run the feed, or see RequestSignerTests for how the signature is
### built. Without a valid signature it answers 401 and writes nothing, which is the point.
POST {{host}}/ingest/events
Content-Type: application/json
X-Pitchwire-Signature: this-will-be-refused
X-Pitchwire-Timestamp: 0

{ "events": [] }

### What a client asks for after its socket dropped, so the timeline has no hole in it. The live feed
### itself is a SignalR hub at /hub/matches, which is not something a request file can speak.
GET {{host}}/matches/REPLACE_WITH_A_MATCH_ID/events?from=1

### What the provider remembers sending for a match, from a given sequence onwards. This is the half a
### push feed cannot do on its own, and it is how a gap gets closed.
GET {{feed}}/matches/REPLACE_WITH_A_MATCH_ID/events?from=1
